# The one list of fleet commands.
#
# `cti-agent` reads it to print its command list and a command's usage, and the
# bash and zsh completions read it at TAB time, so there is nothing generated
# to go stale. Installed as $CODE_DIR/commands.manifest.
#
# A test (test_lanes.py, TestCommandManifest) fails the build when a command's
# real flags, or a command in cmd/ or fleet-kit/fleet/bin, differ from this
# file. Add a flag or a command there and here in the same change.
#
# One stanza per command, blank line between:
#   command: NAME
#   about:   one line, what it is for
#   runs:    read   changes nothing
#            local  writes only its own output and state files
#            effect sends mail, moves mail, files tickets or changes another system
#   flag:    --NAME [ARG] | what it does       (ARG is a placeholder, not a value)
#   sub:     NAME | what it does                (first word after the command)
#
#   modes:   (effect commands only) the flags that say how to run it; exactly one is
#            required, and with none the command prints usage and exits 2. --to-dev
#            may also go with --dry-run.
#   risk:    FLAG safe|writes|changes - how the completions colour a flag: safe is
#            read-only or a rehearsal, writes touches only the fleet's own files
#            or a test send, changes alters something outside. The mode flags
#            (--dry-run safe, --to-dev writes, --for-real changes), --check, and
#            every flag of a read command are classed already; list the rest here.
#   exempt:  flags that make an effect command read-only, so no mode is needed
#            (cti-jira --check, cti-mailer --check and --armed, mailer.py --check)

command: brief.py
about: Render the CTI digest from an enriched file
runs: local
flag: --enriched FILE | enriched JSON from enrich.py
flag: --daily | the daily digest
flag: --weekly | the weekly digest
flag: --subject-only | print the subject line only
flag: --out FILE | HTML output path
flag: --text-out FILE | plain-text output path

command: cti-alert
about: Tell the operator a unit failed or the heartbeat needs a person
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real --to-dev
flag: --dry-run | print what would be sent, send and post nothing
flag: --kind STRING | headline word: FAILED for a crash, HOLD for a deliberate stop, ESCALATION when the orchestrator needs a human and nothing is broken. TEST is inferred when the named unit is healthy (default "FAILED")
flag: --reason STRING | one-line explanation shown above the systemd detail
flag: --to-dev | send to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only, subject prefixed [DEV TEST], and post nothing to the board
flag: --unit STRING | the systemd unit that failed (required)
flag: --for-real | send the alert and post to the board

command: cti-appreport
about: Build each application's report for the owner emails, and fetch the scanner's own report for the tickets
runs: local
flag: --app-id STRING | fetch the report for this one web application id (a test)
flag: --app-name STRING | with --app-id: the name used in messages and the report title
flag: --check | make one cheap authenticated call, print the scanner's rate-limit position, and exit
flag: --deliveries STRING | the owners lane: build the owner report from the scanner's API (one finding search per application, one knowledge-base lookup per 1,000 kinds of finding) for every application in this cti-appscan --deliveries-out file that is not held, and write each covering email beside it in --out-dir with send.tsv and held.tsv for the runner
flag: --dump-request | with --app-id: print the request that creates the report, send nothing
flag: --keep | leave each report in the scanner instead of deleting it (debugging)
flag: --list-ids | print the web application id, name and findings of every application in the newest $FLEET_HOME/state/appscan-tickets-<date>.json (or the --tickets file), and exit; reads the file only - no scanner calls, no credentials
flag: --max-reports INT | fetch at most this many reports in one run; the rest are listed as not fetched (default 30)
flag: --no-fetch | with --deliveries: write the covering emails without fetching any report (a dry run)
flag: --only-ref STRING | with --tickets: a cti-jira --ref-out preview; fetch only the applications whose ticket would be filed or commented (would-create, would-comment)
flag: --out-dir STRING | write each report (<label>.zip) and reports.json here, 0600; created 0700 if missing
flag: --owner-report STRING | build the owner report for this one web application id and write it with its covering email to --out-dir, then exit: a preview of the attachment; nothing is mailed or recorded
flag: --probe-findings STRING | print the scanner's verbose finding search for this web application id (3 open vulnerabilities), with Cookie, Set-Cookie and Authorization redacted, and exit
flag: --request-file STRING | send this create-report body instead of the built-in one ({{APP_ID}} and {{NAME}} are filled in) - for correcting a field name while probing, without a rebuild
flag: --tickets STRING | fetch a report for every application in this cti-appscan --tickets-out file that has findings and was not skipped
flag: --timeout DURATION | give up on one report after this long (default 10m0s)

command: cti-appscan
about: Read the scan notices and render the AppSec weekly
runs: local
risk: --commit-delivered writes
risk: --commit-seen writes
risk: --stamp-tickets writes
flag: --auth-fail-out STRING | write one line per scan whose configured credential failed to authenticate; the runner alerts the operator when this file is non-empty. Absent or empty means every credential worked.
flag: --commit-delivered STRING | record this file's key<TAB>ref<TAB>status lines in --delivered-log, then exit
flag: --commit-seen STRING | record a --seen-out file in the processed log the mailbox cleanup reads, so those notifications can be archived; then exit
flag: --delivered-log STRING | the owners lane's record of the last scan sent or held per application
flag: --deliveries-out STRING | write which applications' scanner reports to mail to their owners (and which are held, and why) here, for cti-appreport --deliveries; scans already in --delivered-log are left out
flag: --list-apps | print a complete app-owners.txt for every web app defined in the scanner - owners already on file kept, new apps with a placeholder - and exit
flag: --json | with --list-apps: print the machine-readable listing fleet-owners sync reads (id, name and URL host as separate fields, and the live websites no web app scans)
flag: --no-detail | skip the Qualys WAS API; report counts from the notifications only
flag: --out STRING | write the HTML here
flag: --seen-out STRING | write the scan notifications this report covered here, for --commit-seen after the report has been sent
flag: --since DURATION | how far back to look for scan notifications (default 168h0m0s)
flag: --stamp-tickets STRING | stamp the cti-jira --ref-out FILE into the --out and --text-out reports already written, using the --tickets-out file, then exit
flag: --subject-only | print the subject line and exit (a full run: every scanner call is made again - the runner uses --subject-out instead)
flag: --subject-out STRING | also write the subject line here, so the runner need not run a second time (and repeat every scanner call) just to learn it
flag: --text-out STRING | write the plain-text version here
flag: --ticket-slot | leave a slot in the report for --stamp-tickets to fill with each application's ticket
flag: --tickets-out STRING | write one Jira ticket's input per application (active findings of Serious or above, with where, risk and fix) here, for cti-jira --appscan-tickets

command: cti-budget
about: The heartbeat's share of the Claude subscription: gate and ledger
runs: local
sub: check | exit 0 to proceed, 3 to skip this beat
sub: record | record a beat after it ran
sub: status | what it would do and why

command: cti-domains
about: Probe the domain inventory and render the weekly report
runs: local
risk: --no-state-write safe
risk: --stamp-ticket writes
flag: --check | read-only: check the inventory can be read and the GoDaddy token works, print counts, and exit
flag: --actions-out STRING | write the DNS changes each domain needs as JSON, for cti-jira --domains-actions
flag: --concurrency INT | domains probed at once (default 8)
flag: --csv-out STRING | write every probed host as CSV (sitecheck.go's columns first)
flag: --inventory STRING | the domain list (one per line, "send" marks a sender) (default "/etc/cti-agent/domains.txt")
flag: --no-registrar | skip GoDaddy; DNS and web checks only
flag: --no-state-write | read last week's state but do not replace it (dry runs)
flag: --out STRING | write the HTML here
flag: --retries INT | retries for a web request that got no response at all (default 1)
flag: --stamp-ticket STRING | stamp the cti-jira --ref-out FILE into the --out and --text-out reports already written, then exit; a missing or unreadable FILE says the status is unavailable
flag: --state STRING | last week's observations, for the diff (default "/Users/jleggett/state/domains-state.json")
flag: --subject-out STRING | write the subject line here (so the runner need not probe twice)
flag: --text-out STRING | write the plain-text version here
flag: --ticket-slot | leave a slot beside the DNS changes for --stamp-ticket to fill with the lock-down ticket
flag: --timeout DURATION | per-request web timeout (default 10s)
flag: --websites-out STRING | write the domains with a live website of their own (not landers, parked pages, redirects or domains marked landing) as JSON, for the AppSec lane's coverage check

command: cti-jira
about: File and update the fleet's Jira tickets
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real
exempt: --check
flag: --approve | also file the findings that would otherwise wait for a human: Sev5 findings that are not on CISA KEV
flag: --appscan-close-test STRING | preview, or with --for-real close, ONE ticket filed by --appscan-test (label cti-agent-test): the single labelled real test of how the weekly run closes the ticket of a retired application. Needs JIRA_ALLOW_CLOSE; refuses any other ticket
flag: --appscan-comment-probe STRING | with --appscan-tickets and --appscan-test: post the weekly comment for that application onto this TEST ticket (filed by --appscan-test) and, if the gateway refuses it, bisect it by line to name the refused text. Preview unless --for-real
flag: --appscan-reports STRING | with --appscan-tickets: a cti-appreport --out-dir; each application's zipped scanner report listed in its reports.json is attached when its ticket is filed or its findings change
flag: --appscan-test STRING | with --appscan-tickets: preview, or with --for-real file, ONE clearly-labelled test ticket for this scanner web-app id - what a developer would get, with the report from --appscan-reports attached; unassigned, never tracked by the weekly run
flag: --appscan-tickets STRING | file or update ONE ticket per application from this cti-appscan --tickets-out JSON, assigned to the owner in app-owners.txt
flag: --check | read-only: verify credentials, project, issue type and required fields
flag: --domains-actions STRING | file the DNS changes in this cti-domains --actions-out JSON as ONE lock-down ticket, or comment on the one already open when the list has changed
flag: --follow-up | with --from-enriched: also re-check every open fleet ticket against the scanner, whether or not its CVE is in today's mail, and comment on spread, on reaching zero, or once on closed-but-still-detected
flag: --for-real | write to Jira: create, comment, close
flag: --from-enriched STRING | file tickets for the present KEV, Sev5 and CVSS 9.0+ findings in this enriched JSON
flag: --ref-out STRING | with --domains-actions: write what happened to the lock-down ticket (key, status, action) here as JSON, for cti-domains --stamp-ticket; with --appscan-tickets, one entry per application for cti-appscan --stamp-tickets
flag: --test-ticket | build a clearly-labelled test ticket
flag: --dry-run | show what would be written to Jira, write nothing

command: cti-kev
about: CISA KEV remediation deadlines for CVEs present in the estate
runs: read
flag: --enriched STRING | path to enriched JSON; default is the newest in $FLEET_HOME/state
flag: --fail-if-overdue | exit 2 when something is overdue (for CI, not for systemd)
flag: --horizon INT | "due soon" window in days (default 14)
flag: --hosts | include sample hostnames; the output then names vulnerable machines
flag: --json | machine-readable output
flag: --quiet | print nothing when nothing is overdue or due within the horizon

command: cti-mailbox
about: Tidy the shared CTI mailbox: archive, delete auto-replies, leave the rest
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real
flag: --backlog-threshold INT | report when this many inbox messages have no processing record. A growing backlog is what 'the agent stopped reading the mailbox' looks like from outside (default 25)
flag: --dry-run | show the plan and move nothing
flag: --for-real | move the mail the plan lists
flag: --json STRING | write the plan here, so what happened can be checked afterwards
flag: --lookback DURATION | how far back to look this run, e.g. 720h for a one-off sweep of old auto-replies; 0 means MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS. Archiving still needs a processing record, and records expire after 14 days
flag: --timeout DURATION | overall Graph timeout (default 2m0s)

command: cti-mailer
about: Send mail through Graph, or check the sender's credentials
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real --to-dev
exempt: --check --armed
flag: --app-id STRING | with --lane owner: the scanner's web app id whose app-owners.txt line names the recipients
flag: --app-name STRING | with --lane owner: the application's name, for an app-owners.txt line keyed by name
flag: --approve | the operator approved this specific off-cycle send
flag: --armed | with --lane owner: exit 0 if the lane has a domain allowlist (owners.db, or OWNER_ALLOW_DOMAINS without one), 3 if not, 1 if the owners cannot be read; send nothing
flag: --attach VALUE | file to attach (repeatable, keep under ~3MB total)
flag: --board-id STRING | board line id, so your reply can be matched back to the question
flag: --cc STRING | comma-separated; defaults to DIGEST_CC. Subject to the same VM_ALLOW_TO gate as --to
flag: --check | verify configuration, token and granted roles
flag: --dry-run | render and validate, send nothing
flag: --from-mailbox STRING | sending mailbox; defaults to GRAPH_MAILBOX
flag: --html STRING | rendered digest to send
flag: --lane STRING | send as a named lane (e.g. was): uses <LANE>_TO and <LANE>_ALLOW_TO, and refuses if either is unset rather than falling back to the digest audience
flag: --message STRING | short plain-text body, wrapped in minimal HTML - for escalations, not digests
flag: --require-approval | refuse unless --approve is also passed (off-cycle sends)
flag: --save-to-sent STRING | keep a copy in Sent Items (default "true")
flag: --subject STRING | subject line
flag: --text STRING | plain-text alternative. Accepted and logged; Graph sends one body, so this is not transmitted. Kept because the runners pass it
flag: --to STRING | comma-separated; defaults to DIGEST_TO
flag: --to-dev | send ONLY to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL, never CC'd, subject prefixed [DEV TEST]: for previewing a report in a real mail client during development
flag: --to-operator | send to FLEET_OPERATOR_EMAIL. Pre-approved: telling the operator something is not an outward-facing send.
flag: --for-real | send the message

command: cti-patchtuesday
about: Build, save and mark the monthly Patch Tuesday synopsis
runs: local
risk: --mark-sent writes
flag: --drop-dir STRING | folder holding saved copies of the two articles (a file named *qualys* and one named *bleeping*, .html or .txt), used instead of fetching; default $FLEET_HOME/inbox/patchtuesday/YYYY-MM
flag: --dry-run | print the text version to stdout, write nothing
flag: --explain STRING | print every place a CVE was found on the source pages, and whether anything ties it to a Microsoft product, then exit
flag: --json STRING | write the structured digest here, for inspection
flag: --mark-sent | record that this month's synopsis was delivered, and exit. The daily digest holds back a release's CVEs only once this is set, so the runner calls it after the mailer succeeds and never before
flag: --max-rows INT | cap the present-CVE table (default 25). The full list is always in --json
flag: --month STRING | YYYY-MM to report on; default is the most recent release. Use this to replay a past month and check the lane against an email you already sent
flag: --no-manifest | do not write the release manifest. run-patchtuesday --dry-run passes this, because a manifest is a statement about what was sent
flag: --out STRING | write the HTML here
flag: --provider STRING | 'none' to skip Qualys entirely
flag: --subject-only | print the subject line and exit
flag: --text-out STRING | write the plain-text version here
flag: --timeout DURATION | per-request HTTP timeout (default 45s)
flag: --url-bleeping STRING | override the BleepingComputer URL
flag: --url-msrc STRING | override Microsoft's release-document URL (the CVRF API); 'none' to skip it
flag: --url-qualys STRING | override the Qualys review URL

command: cti-podprobe
about: Sample the Proofpoint on Demand log stream
runs: read
flag: --idle DURATION | stop when no data arrives for this long (default 1m30s)
flag: --json | print the aggregate as JSON
flag: --max-bytes INT | stop after reading this many bytes (default 209715200)
flag: --since STRING | start of the window, e.g. 2026-11-02T10:00:00-0500 (default: the hour that began 3 hours ago)
flag: --timeout DURATION | give up after this long (default 10m0s)
flag: --to STRING | end of the window (default: one hour after --since)
flag: --top INT | rows to print per table (default 20)
flag: --type STRING | stream type: message, maillog or audit (default "message")

command: cti-supplier
about: Supplier-request lane: find new supplier requests, judge them, file Kanban items, write the digest
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real
risk: --collect safe
risk: --finish changes
risk: --commit-seen writes
risk: --commit-owned writes
flag: --dry-run | read and judge, say what would be filed, write nothing to Jira or the record of what was reported
flag: --for-real | with --finish: file the Kanban items; with --commit-seen: record the requests as reported; with --commit-owned: record the settled mail for the mailbox cleanup
flag: --collect DIR | find new supplier requests and write what the model needs into this directory
flag: --finish DIR | judge the requests in this directory, file the Kanban items and write the digest
flag: --commit-seen FILE | record a seen.json from --finish so the same requests are not reported again, then exit
flag: --commit-owned FILE | record an owned.json from --finish in the processed log the mailbox cleanup reads, so the mail of requests dealt with is filed in Archive; then exit
flag: --since DURATION | how far back to look for requests (default SUPPLIER_WINDOW_DAYS days, else 3)

command: cti-vmscan
about: Combine the week's Qualys VM scan-result notices into one report
runs: local
risk: --commit-seen writes
flag: --check | read-only: check the Graph credentials, the network spreadsheet and the Qualys Asset Group list the coverage check needs, print counts, and exit
flag: --since DURATION | how far back to look for scan-result notices
flag: --scans STRING | comma-separated titles of the scheduled scans to combine (default VMSCAN_SCANS, else the two weeklies)
flag: --out FILE | write the HTML here
flag: --text-out FILE | write the plain-text version here
flag: --subject-out FILE | write the subject line here
flag: --seen-out FILE | write the notices this report covered here, for --commit-seen after the send
flag: --no-coverage | skip the check of the network spreadsheet against the Asset Groups the scans cover
flag: --subnets-file FILE | read the network spreadsheet (.xlsx) from this file instead of SharePoint, for testing
flag: --csv-out FILE | write the coverage findings as a worksheet (CSV) here, for the runner to attach: one row per network not scanned or spreadsheet row to fix, with its sheet row and a blank column for the teams to say why
flag: --commit-seen FILE | record a --seen-out file in the processed log the mailbox cleanup reads, then exit

command: enrich.py
about: Enrich CVEs with NVD, EPSS and KEV
runs: local
flag: --report FILE | markdown report from cti-agent
flag: --cves LIST | comma-separated CVE list (scout lane)
flag: --out FILE | output JSON path
flag: --no-db | skip the SQLite upsert
flag: --skip-nvd | EPSS and KEV only (fast)

command: fleet-board
about: The append-only message board between the agents and the operator
runs: local
sub: post | append one line: FROM TO KIND BODY
sub: say | append one line from @you
sub: read | lines for a handle
sub: resolve | archive a question and its answers
sub: expire | archive lines older than N days
sub: prune | archive lines matching a pattern (operator only)
sub: tail | last N lines

command: fleet-db
about: SQLite memory: findings, tasks, mail, sent digests
runs: local
sub: init | create the schema
sub: remember | store a memory
sub: recent | recent memories, open tasks, unacked mail
sub: task | add, done or list tasks
sub: mail | send, inbox or ack board mail
sub: finding | upsert a finding
sub: findings | list findings
sub: rollup | summarise the findings
sub: sent | record a digest as delivered
sub: note | record who is handling a finding
sub: was-sent | exit 0 if a digest already went out
sub: vacuum | compact the database

command: fleet-journal
about: Read the journal of cti-agent units (the orchestrator's only way to it)
runs: read
flag: -u UNIT | a cti-agent unit; repeatable
flag: --unit UNIT | the same as -u
flag: --since WHEN | start of the window
flag: -n N | number of lines
flag: --lines N | the same as -n
flag: --no-pager | do not page

command: fleet-owners
about: Who owns each web application, kept in SQLite (runs as root)
runs: local
sub: init | create the database
sub: import | load an owners file
sub: list | every application
sub: show | one application
sub: check | validate the data
sub: history | the change log
sub: names | fill application names from a listing
sub: set | set an owner
sub: rm | remove an application
sub: watcher | add or remove watchers
sub: covers | add or remove covered hosts
sub: site | set or remove a website owner
sub: domain | allow, remove or list owner domains
sub: export | write owners.json
sub: snapshot | write the snapshot
sub: backup | copy the database

command: fleet-trace
about: What each heartbeat did: tools, denials, turns, cost (state/traces)
runs: local
sub: show | print one beat as a timeline (--last, --full)
sub: list | the latest beats, one line each (-n N)
sub: prune | delete traces older than --days N
sub: init | create the trace directory
sub: tee | used by run-checkin: record a beat from stdin

command: fleet-timers
about: Whether a timer has been disabled, which raises no alarm on its own
runs: read
flag: -a | the same as --all
flag: --all | every cti-agent timer, not just the scheduled ones

command: mailer.py
about: Send the digest or an escalation through Graph
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real
exempt: --check
flag: --html FILE | HTML body
flag: --message TEXT | short plain-text body for escalations
flag: --text FILE | plain-text alternative (logged only)
flag: --subject TEXT | subject line
flag: --to LIST | comma-separated recipients, default DIGEST_TO
flag: --cc LIST | comma-separated cc, default DIGEST_CC
flag: --to-operator | send to FLEET_OPERATOR_EMAIL
flag: --from-mailbox ADDR | send as this mailbox
flag: --attach FILE | attach a file; repeatable
flag: --board-id ID | board line id to match a reply to
flag: --dry-run | build and validate, send nothing
flag: --require-approval | refuse unless --approve is also given
flag: --approve | the operator approved this off-cycle send
flag: --save-to-sent MODE | keep a copy in Sent Items
flag: --check | verify credentials and granted roles
flag: --for-real | send the message

command: run-appowners
about: Daily application scan reports to their owners
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real --to-dev
flag: --dry-run | render and print, send and change nothing
flag: --to-dev | every email to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only
flag: --for-real | send each owner their report and record it

command: run-appscan
about: Weekly application-security report
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real --to-dev
flag: --dry-run | render and print, send and change nothing
flag: --to-dev | test send to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only
flag: --for-real | send the report and file or update the tickets

command: run-checkin
about: The heartbeat: a short orchestrator beat that watches the fleet and escalates
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real
flag: --audit | checks only, no beat (the older name for --dry-run)
flag: --for-real | fire one beat
flag: --dry-run | checks only, no beat (the same as --audit)

command: run-digest
about: Daily or weekly CTI digest: read the mailbox, enrich, render and send
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real --to-dev
flag: --dry-run | render and print, send and change nothing
flag: --to-dev | test send to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only
flag: --lookback DURATION | how far back to read the mailbox
flag: --for-real | send the digest and record it
sub: daily | the morning digest
sub: weekly | the Monday weekly

command: run-domains
about: Weekly domains check: probe, render, file the lock-down ticket, send
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real --to-dev
flag: --dry-run | render and print, send and change nothing
flag: --to-dev | test send to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only
flag: --for-real | send the report, file the lock-down ticket, advance the baseline

command: run-mailbox-cleanup
about: Daily tidy of the shared CTI mailbox
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real
flag: --dry-run | print the plan and the reason for every message, move nothing
flag: --for-real | move the mail the plan lists

command: run-patchtuesday
about: Monthly Patch Tuesday synopsis
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real --to-dev
flag: --dry-run | render and print, send and change nothing
flag: --to-dev | test send to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only
flag: --month YYYY-MM | a past release: --dry-run replays it; --for-real needs FLEET_CONFIRM_RESEND=MONTH too
flag: --for-real | send the synopsis and mark it sent
flag: --resend | with --for-real: send a release that was already sent

command: run-supplier
about: Supplier-request lane: judge each new supplier request, file a Kanban item for the critical or unsure ones, email one digest
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real --to-dev
flag: --dry-run | judge and render, file and send nothing, record nothing
flag: --for-real | judge, file the Kanban items, send the digest, record the requests as reported and their mail for the cleanup
flag: --to-dev | test send of the digest to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only; no items filed

command: run-vmscan
about: Weekly Qualys VM scan report: the two scheduled scans combined into one email
runs: effect
modes: --dry-run --for-real --to-dev
flag: --dry-run | render and print the subject, send and record nothing
flag: --for-real | send the report and record the notices as used
flag: --to-dev | test send to FLEET_DEV_EMAIL only

command: scout.py
about: Poll the CTI feeds for new CVE IDs
runs: local
flag: --out FILE | output path
flag: --feeds FILE | feed list
flag: --days N | ignore entries older than this
flag: --print-new | print new CVEs on stdout
flag: --no-db | skip the SQLite record
